  "step_2": "The individual reports having looked at the probabilities and decided to go with the option that offers a higher probability of winning. The individual focuses on the likelihood of obtaining a positive (winning) outcome, explicitly valuing a 'higher probability for a win'. The justification centers on the chance of avoiding the zero outcome, not on the magnitude of the prize or other distributional features like skewness per se. The deliberation is outcome-oriented toward securing any win, emphasizing probability of gain over amount gained.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on comparing the skewness of the lotteries, interpreted as the relative concentration of probability on favorable outcomes. In this case, that aligns with comparing the probabilities of winning (i.e., getting a positive amount). The individual considers exactly this: the probability of winning, preferring the lottery with the higher chance of a non-zero outcome (A has 25% vs B's 20%). Although the term 'skewness' is not used, the individual is effectively responding to the same informational basis—probability mass on favorable outcomes—and makes a choice consistent with the reason's rule. The justification ('higher probability for a win') maps directly to the core consideration of the decision reason. Therefore, it is highly likely the individual used a reasoning process aligned with the skewness-based reason, even if not labeled as such.",
  "final_assessment_score": 95
