  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating the skewness of the outcome distribution—specifically, whether more probability mass is on favorable versus unfavorable outcomes. The relevant comparison is that Lottery B has a more favorable skew (99.9% on 0 vs 0.1% on loss) than A (99.8% on 0 vs 0.2% on loss). However, the individual does not mention or appear to consider the relative concentration of probability on favorable outcomes. Instead, the reasoning focuses on a trade-off between the size and likelihood of losses, suggesting a heuristic like 'better to lose less even if slightly more likely.' This is not equivalent to assessing skewness. There is no evidence that the individual evaluated the distributional asymmetry or compared the ratios of favorable to unfavorable probabilities. Therefore, while both the reason and the individual consider probabilities and outcomes, the conceptual basis (skewness vs. loss magnitude-probability trade-off) differs. The individual’s justification does not align with the rule of preferring the more left-skewed lottery.",
