{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more left-skewed (i.e., has a higher concentration of probability on favorable outcomes). To evaluate skewness, we compare the distribution of outcomes and their probabilities. Lottery A has two outcomes: 1000 EUR with 50% probability and 0 EUR with 50% probability. This is a symmetric distribution around its mean (500 EUR), so it is not skewed. Lottery B offers 500 EUR with 100% probability, which is a degenerate distribution (no variance), and thus also not skewed in the traditional sense. However, since it guarantees the expected value of Lottery A, it can be interpreted as neutral or minimally skewed. Neither lottery is clearly left- or right-skewed. Still, the reason can be applied in a comparative sense: if one were more left-skewed than the other, it would be preferred. In this case, both lotteries are symmetric or deterministic, so the reason would likely conclude indifference or a weak preference depending on interpretation. Nonetheless, the relevant information (outcomes and probabilities) is available, so the reason can be applied.",
